Subject: Power calculations with non-parametric bootstrap
Message-ID: <200505021854.j42IsBR20783@drac.host4u.net>

Hello,

 

I need to estimate 
the power of a test for gene selection based on a rank-based criterion as a 
function of sample size using data from a pilot study.  The pilot study has 
N=200 samples and I would like to estimate the power using using bootstrap-based 
simulation for sample sizes n*=20,50,100,200 of the original N 
samples.

 

There are at 
least two different ways of doing this using non-parametric 
bootstrap:

 

(1) Sample with 
replacement n* samples from the N original data points and use this 
dataset in the function "boot" with R=200 to obtain the bootstrap 
distribution of the statistic for sample size n*.

 

(2) Draw with 
replacement one sample of size n* from the original N 
samples.  Calculate the statistic based on the n* samples.  
Draw another sample of size n* and repeat the process 200 
times.

 

Although both 
methods provide an estimate of the sampling variability of the test statistic, 
the difference is that the first method does not sample efficiently 
the variability within the pilot data.  Therefore I think method 
2 should be preferred. 

 

The bootstrap 
samples generated by the function "boot" are of the same size as the 
original dataset (i.e. it draws with replacement n*=N samples from the original 
N samples).  I was wondering if anybody knows a way (using "boot" or 
another bootstrap function) to draw a different set of size n*<N in each 
bootstrap replication and to evaluate the statistics based on these sets.  
Is a "bootstrap-within-bootstrap" approach the only 
possibility? 

 

I appreciate your 
time.

Thank 
you.

 

-Christos

 

