References: <200505021854.j42IsBR20783@drac.host4u.net>
Message-ID: <SE2KEXCH01eIiGXix4A0000008b@se2kexch01.insightful.com>

Christos Hatzis wrote:

The bootstrap() function in S+Resample (see below) supports sampling
with different size, e.g.
        bootstrap(data, statistic,
                  sampler = samp.bootstrap(size = 20))

I'm leery of your version (2); depending what quantities you calculate
from each bootstrap sample, you could end up with much more variability
than is appropriate.

I suggest instead doing sensitivity analysis using reweight(), which
provides a "Diagnostic procedure, to estimate how a bootstrap distribution
would change if sampling had been from a different distribution (with
the same values, but different probabilities on those values)."
You can choose the weights to perturb the original data in various
weights, e.g. larger variance or larger mean.  Tools provided to
assist with this include the tilt.after.bootstrap(), tiltWeights(), and
resampGetL() functions.

Tim Hesterberg

