From alan.hochberg at prosanos.com  Tue May  3 14:22:11 2005
From: alan.hochberg at prosanos.com (Alan Hochberg)
Date: Tue, 03 May 2005 10:22:11 -0400
Subject: Re: Soliciting ideas [Fitting a formula to data from machines]
In-Reply-To: <3007F52DF96EB74CAC3054FF85C4318F7A7C28@mail2>
References: <3007F52DF96EB74CAC3054FF85C4318F7A7C28@mail2>
Message-ID: <20050503142246.2A7881008539@mailgate.biostat.wustl.edu>

The first thing to realize is that there
are two sources of deviation between the machine results and the formula.  The
first is systematic error.  In other words, the shape of the formula’s
curve doesn’t match the corresponding shape that you get from plotting
the data from either machine as you vary its settings.  (I assume there
are settings or operating conditions of some sort, hence the “formula”.)
 The second is random error or noise—irreproducibility in the
machine results that will cause the data to deviate from any smooth curve at
all.  Any of the tests you mentioned will be affected by both of these
sources of deviation—which differ from machine to machine—in a way
that may make the results hard to interpret.

 

One suggestion, if this is practical, is
to perform several runs to obtain several sets of data from each machine, at
each of several operating points corresponding to whatever settings or
parameters which go into your formula.  Then you can look at the
run-to-run variability at each operating point.  A couple of questions to
ask yourself:  Does the amount of variability (noise) change as you vary
the settings/parameters of each machine?  Or is it more or less
constant?  Is one machine noisier than the other?  Does the noise
look normally distributed, or are their outliers?  S-plus is a great
graphical tool.  PLOT the data and make histograms at each operating point
to LOOK AT THE DATA and the noise, before relying on statistical formulas to
produce numbers and test hypothesis.

 

If it’s practical for you to obtain
several runs per machine, you can average them together, hopefully yielding a
smooth curve that you can compare to your formula.  You can easily smooth
the curve with loess regression.  Again, it’s much more important to
PLOT your formula and the machine data on a graph, and LOOK at the differences
in shape, for each machine.  This will be much more informative than
simply getting a number, and is very easy in S-plus.  You can certainly use
mean square deviation if your data is reasonably normal and free of outliers.  As
far as which machine is “best”, it’s a little more
complicated.  Would you rather have a machine that follows the formula
more closely, if it throws an occasional outlier or has more noise?  It
depends on what you’re using the machine for, of course.  The web is
a great resource for experimental data analysis, and if you were in my office
right now I’d reach for “Statistics for Experimenters” by
Box, Hunter, and Hunter.  In the meantime, this three-paragraph course on experimental
data analysis is brought to you by…

 

