From timh at insightful.com  Thu May  5 19:05:32 2005
From: timh at insightful.com (Tim Hesterberg)
Date: Thu, 05 May 2005 12:05:32 -0700
Subject: Re: Smoothing and disaggregating
Message-ID: <SE2KEXCH01LqJrfP6nm000005dd@se2kexch01.insightful.com>

I suggest using bs() or ns().
Use one of these to create a basis.  Then solve for the coefficients
that satisfy the constraints, that averages of 6 values give specified
values.  E.g.
        bs(x, knots, Bound=T) %*% coefficients
gives predictions at x; you need to find coefficients.

If you use too many knots, then there will not be a unique solution.
In that case, you could choose the coefficients that minimize the
squared integrated second derivative of the smooth curve, subject
to the constraints.

With a set of knots of the right size, there is only one solution.
There may be a set of knots of that size that yields the globally-optimum
solution (minimal integral of f''^2 subject to the constraints).

Tim Hesterberg

