Subject: Subdividing pairs plots
Message-ID: <427B7B68.5040000@imap.iu.edu>

Suppose I have a dataframe with twenty columns of numerical values, 
v1--v20.  I’d like a pairs plot, to see each variable plotted against 
each other, but a 20x20 comes out too small to be useful.  Has anyone 
worked out an easy way to break a plot like this into three or four, 
with pairs of v1--v10 in one, pairs of v11--v20 in another, and pairs of 
v1--v10 plotted against v11--v20 in a third?  (I wouldn’t care if the 
reflection of that last set were not produced.)

Thanks for any suggestions.

Dave Parkhurst

From rmh at temple.edu  Fri May  6 14:54:30 2005
From: rmh at temple.edu (Richard M. Heiberger)
Date: Fri, 06 May 2005 10:54:30 -0400
Subject: Re: Subdividing pairs plots
Message-ID: <4270d2bc.f83ff5c7.81a0400@po-d.temple.edu>

I recommend the xysplom function that we provide as part of the library
associated with our book

                Statistical Analysis and Data Display
                Richard M. Heiberger and Burt Holland

Full details of the book are available at the Springer site
   http://springeronline.com/0-387-40270-5
The website also points to the downloadable online files that accompany the 
book.

We will be giving a short course at the Joint Statistics Meetings this summer in
Minneapolis on Monday, August 8, 2005.

For your example (slightly simplified), use the sequence

abc <- data.frame(matrix(rnorm(80), 10))
names(abc) <- c("v1","v2","v3","v4","v5","v6","v7","v8")
xysplom(v1+v2+v3+v4 ~ v1+v2+v3+v4, data=abc)
xysplom(v1+v2+v3+v4 ~ v5+v6+v7+v8, data=abc)
xysplom(v5+v6+v7+v8 ~ v1+v2+v3+v4, data=abc)
xysplom(v5+v6+v7+v8 ~ v5+v6+v7+v8, data=abc)

