From alan.hochberg at prosanos.com  Tue May 10 17:34:07 2005
From: alan.hochberg at prosanos.com (Alan Hochberg)
Date: Tue, 10 May 2005 13:34:07 -0400
Subject: Re: Off-topic: Anocova of change scores with baseline
In-Reply-To: <C4A57662D47C7B44B781D39E4C8F0694024CFACC@SAIGA.win.uthscsa.edu>
References: <C4A57662D47C7B44B781D39E4C8F0694024CFACC@SAIGA.win.uthscsa.edu>
Message-ID: <20050510173440.5DEC11007EF2@mailgate.biostat.wustl.edu>

Dear Joe:

 

Your question generated an interesting
lunchtime discussion with our mathematician, Ron Pearson.  He points out
that the analysis-of-covariance process is generating estimates:

 

Yn – Y0 = aHat * Y0
+ cHat * X + …

 

in the first case, and 

 

Yn = bHat * Y0 + cHat * X + …

 

in the second case.

 

While it is true in your formulation that
b=1+a, it is NOT true that the estimated quantities will satisfy bHat = 1 +
aHat.  The reason is that the error components, which the analysis algorithm
is attempting to minimize, differ between the two cases.  In the first
case, the algorithm is trying to find estimates for a and c that give the best
fit to the Yn-Y0 data.  In the second case, the algorithm is trying to
find estimates for b and c that give the best fit to the Yn data.  Thus there
isn’t a simple algebraic relationship between a and b.

 

Please let me know if I can provide
further information.

 

Best regards,

 

Alan

 

