>
> because b = (1+a).
>
> Is this a new approach in clinical trials? What am I missing?
>
> Joe
>
> Joseph F. Lucke, PhD

I see such analyses of change scores used quite often in pharmaceutical 
clinical trials.  It represents a basic misunderstanding, as Stephen 
Senn has written about in his book Statistical Issues in Drug 
Development.  Change scores have a number of undersirable properties 
related to measurement error, regression to the mean, ceiling effects, 
etc.  It's best to analyze the final response without taking the change.

