Message-ID: <Pine.GSO.4.60.0505111935440.8530@hcp>

This is a consequence most likely of the S scoping rules.  Your little
test case defines offset in position 1 (the working database) which
comes ahead of the database where the built-in function offset() is
stored.  (Print it; the world's most boring function.)
So when you refer to it in the function defined within sapply() you
find the right value.

When you use this expression within a larger program, offset is not
necessarily in database position 1; it might be in a frame (other than
0 or 1) if you used this within another function.  Then the function
offset() is in the search path but the number variable is not, so you
get the error of trying to subtract a function from a number.

This is one of the most commonly recurring issues we see on this list and
it occurs with some standard modeling functions that are written to assume
that certain objects will be found in the search path from within the
function when they are not necessarily going to be there.

On Wed, 11 May 2005, Leeds, Mark wrote:
> Can someone explain the following to me ?
> I tested the following at the Splus command line.
> changepoints<-c(64,270)
> offset<-19
> decimate<-sapply(changepoints,function(i)(i-offset):(i-1)
