Message-ID: <20050512145019.1FD9E1005F1F@mailgate.biostat.wustl.edu>

We should keep in mind that many of the
users of this list are trying to be on the forefront of statistics, conversant
in IT and software concepts (like floating-point arithmetic), and at the same
time, to develop and utilize subject matter expertise in their own field, which
may be medicine, finance, ecology, or some similar area of intellectual pursuit
that takes a lifetime to master on its own.  It’s a tall
order.  Add to that the S-plus language and system, with fantastic
capabilities, but also with some features that could charitably be described as
“quirky”.  Furthermore, there’s not a lot of local
S-plus expertise available most places.  Chances are the person down the
hall knows C# or Java.

 

Yes, we get the occasional lazy questioner
who writes to the list instead of typing “?lm”.  But most of
us are diligent, and we’re experts in SOMETHING, it just may not be
S-plus.

 

One of the charms of this list is being
able to ask a question, and get an answer from a Dr. Harrell or a Dr. Ripley,
who literally “wrote the book”.  But there’s a wide variance
in knowledge here, about a system that’s complex, not universally
deployed, and used by statistics/IT outsiders as well as insiders.  I for
one want to raise the mean level of S expertise, both by asking questions on
this list, and answering them when I can.

 

Alan Hochberg

(Opinions are mine, and are subject to
change over time.)

