If your funct1 returns a vector of the same length for every call, then 
 apply will return a matrix (or array) with the value of funct1 along 
the  first dimension (no matter which dimension that was in the original 
array).

Note that if your funct1 actually returns a matrix, that value is just 
treated as a vector (the dimension information is ignored and discarded).

It's difficult to respond to the statement "... doesn't give the desired 
answer" as the distance-mind-reading module on my computer seems to be 
malfunctioning at the moment.  And I also don't know whether you really 
meant to allude to the "aperm" function, rather than some "perm" function.

Here are some examples.  The first one is my best guess at what your 
"desired output" might be, but the call is same as what you say doesn't 
give "desired output".

> x <- array(0, dim=3:5)
> dim(aperm(apply(x, c(1,3), c), c(2,1,3)))
[1] 3 4 5
> dim(aperm(apply(x, c(1,2), c), c(2,3,1)))
[1] 3 4 5
> dim(apply(x, c(2,3), c))
[1] 3 4 5
> dim(apply(x, 1, as.matrix))
[1] 20  3
> dim(apply(x, 2, as.matrix))
[1] 15  4
> dim(apply(x, 3, as.matrix))
[1] 12  5
>

Hope this helps,

Tony Plate

