From parkhurs at imap.iu.edu  Mon May 23 18:20:53 2005
From: parkhurs at imap.iu.edu (David Parkhurst)
Date: Mon, 23 May 2005 13:20:53 -0500
Subject: Bootstrap analysis of nested data
In-Reply-To: <001201c55c72$f2de76e0$65fea8c0@RasmusE>
References: <001201c55c72$f2de76e0$65fea8c0@RasmusE>
Message-ID: <42921F05.4020004@imap.iu.edu>

This question involves both the logic of bootstrapping, and whether the 
proposed analysis can be used to provide BCa confidence intervals with 
either of the bootstrap packages available in s-plus.

Here’s a simplified surrogate of our real situation:

Our data are for two sets of forest plots, A and B, with four plots in 
each set.  We have data for two years.  Set A was not treated between 
year 1 and year 2, but Set B underwent a certain treatment.  Each plot 
contains a number of bird nests;  that number varies from plot to plot 
and from year to year.  We also know for each nest whether it was or was 
not successful in producing young.

The overall success rate p changed from year to year in both sets of 
plots.  We would like to use bootstrapping to obtain confidence limits 
for the ratio of ratios,

RR = [p(B2)/p(B1)]/[p(A2)/p(A1)],
where p(B2) denotes the overall success proportion in the B plots in 
year 2, etc.  The ultimate question is whether the data provide evidence 
that the treatment affected the success rate, given that that rate 
varied from year to year even in the untreated plots.  This is really a 
question about the interaction between year and treatment.

I propose to run a nested bootstrap analysis, with the outer level (for 
one iteration) comprising sampling 4 of the A plots with replacement 
from the A’s, and 4 of the B plots with replacement from the B’s.  For 
each plot chosen, I would choose a single bootstrap sample of nests from 
the year 1 data (with replacement), and a single bootstrap sample of 
nests from the year 2 data.  Then, I would use the results to calculate 
a value of RR.

Finally, I would like to obtain BCa confidence limits for RR.

Two questions:

1. Does this seem an appropriate way to bootstrap with nested data like 
these, and

2. Can sequential bootstrapping like this be used to produce BCa limits, 
using either of the boot libraries available in s-plus 6 or 7 (under 
windows XP)?

Thanks for any advice.

David Parkhurst

