Pat,

 

You've done a good job posting this for us! However, I doubt that considering individuals as variables is the right thing to do. Suppose, you have 50 individuals (subjects) involved; you will collect 50 series on each occasion. There is no software available to handle simultaneously 50 time-series. The analysis and interpretation would be extravagant. I agree with you that for each characteristic or attribute the series should be appended, i.e., if you are measuring say three characteristics on four occasions you will have (following your reasoning):

characteristic1, occasion 1: {c111, c112,...,c1110}

characteristic2, occasion 1 : {c211, c212,...,c2110}

characteristic3, occasion 1 : {c311, c312,...,c3110}

:

:

characteristic1, occasion 4 : {c141, c142,...,c1410}

characteristic2, occasion 4 : {c241, c242,...,c2410}

characteristic3, occasion 4 : {c341, c342,...,c3410}

where cijk stands for characteristic i (i=1,2,3) on occasion j (j=1,2,3,4) from individual k (k=1,2,...,10)

 

The time-series at the end of your observation period will be

series on characteristic 1: serie1<-c(c111, c112,...,c1110,...,c141, c142,...,c1410)

series on characteristic 2: serie2<-c(c211, c212,...,c2110,...,c241, c242,...,c2410)

series on characteristic 3: serie3<-c(c311, c312,...,c3110,...,c341, c342,...,c3410)

 

Now you can run multivariate on three series instead of 10. Unfortunately, here stops my contribution; I have no background in mts to help further. However, I would appreciate if you could share any reply. 

 

Cheers,

Alain

