Dave Evens wrote:
> Dear all,
>
>
> I have a list of dataframes from (1 to no.of.subs) -
> some of the column classes have been read incorrectly
> as factors instead of characters. I have
>
> class(dataframes) is a list
> class(dataframes[[k]]) is a data.frame
> class(dataframes[[k]][j]) is a factor or numeric
> (where j runs from 1 to no.of.cols[[k]])
>
> I would like to use the sapply function to change the
> columns that have class factor to character for all
> data frames.
>
> I thought something along the lines of:
>
> dataframes <- sapply(1: no.of.subs, function(k)
> sapply(1:no.of.cols[[k]], function(j) {
>  if(is.factor(dataframes[[k]][,j])) dataframes[[k][,j]
> <- as.character(dataframes[[k]][,j])
>  }
> ))
>
> would work, but doesn't.
>
> Can someone please tell me where I'm going wrong.

"would work, but doesn't" is pretty vague, but I always find correct 
syntax is a good place to start.  In this particular case your statement 
inside the function does not have balanced brackets [square].

However, even if those were fixed (which, presumably, it was when you 
ran it, but "doesn't work" leaves doubt), that code would not produce 
the result you want because sapply won't return what you want (also it 
looks like you are trying to change 'dataframes' both piece by piece 
from within your sapply, and as a whole as the result from sapply, which 
is a construct highly unlikely to be of much use in any circustance). 
(There are also other problems with that code, e.g., 'k' is not visible 
within the inner 'sapply', and you can't replace a column of a global 
dataframe from within a function.)

Here's a way to do what you want:

> dataframes <- list(data.frame(a=1:3, b=LETTERS[1:3], 
stringsAsFactors=F), data.frame(c=4:6,d=letters[4:6]))

> lapply(dataframes, sapply, class)
[[1]]:
         a           b
 "integer" "character"

[[2]]:
         c        d
 "integer" "factor"

> dataframes1 <- lapply(dataframes, function(d) {for (i in 
seq(along=d)) if (is.factor(d[[i]])) d[[i]] <- as.character(d[[i]]); d})

> lapply(dataframes1, sapply, class)
[[1]]:
         a           b
 "integer" "character"

[[2]]:
         c           d
 "integer" "character"

>

Note that it's not straightforward to use an lapply as the inner loop, 
because assigning all the columns of a dataframe using the construct 
data.frame[] <- lapply(...) coerces the character vectors in the result 
of the lapply() to factors, e.g.:

> dataframes2 <- lapply(dataframes, function(d) {d[] <- lapply(d, 
function(col) if (is.factor(col)) as.character(col) else col); d})

> lapply(dataframes2, sapply, class)
[[1]]:
         a        b
 "integer" "factor"

[[2]]:
         c        d
 "integer" "factor"

>

Here's the only way that I found to use an lapply() on the columns of 
the data frames:

> dataframes3 <- lapply(dataframes, function(d) 
data.frame(row.names=row.names(d), lapply(d, function(col) if 
(is.factor(col)) as.character(col) else col), stringsAsFactors=F))

> lapply(dataframes3, sapply, class)
[[1]]:
         a           b
 "integer" "character"

[[2]]:
         c           d
 "integer" "character"

>

hope this helps,

Tony Plate

PS, the widespread tendency of dataframe operations to coerce character 
data to factor data is one reason that people, for some tasks, dislike 
and sometimes avoid dataframes.

> Thanks in advance for any help.
